FINAL ANSWER:
Yes, ownership is heavily concentrated in one person. **Fatih Arslan holds 59.5% of the surviving code by themselves.** I counted it with `git blame -w -M -C` at HEAD across all 11 files: 1,460 of 1,467 non-blank lines could be attributed (1,832 lines including blank ones).

**Top holders by surviving lines:**

| # | Author | Lines (non-blank) | Share | Incl. blank | Commits | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Fatih Arslan | 869 | 59.5% | 1,125 / 61.7% | 80 (57.6%) | 2026-03-20 |
| 2 | Linda_pp | 111 | 7.6% | 136 / 7.5% | 1 | 2017-05-23 |
| 3 | Matthew Hall | 84 | 5.8% | 98 / 5.4% | 1 | 2026-03-04 |
| 4 | Greg Poirson | 49 | 3.4% | 64 / 3.5% | 7 | 2023-10-17 |
| 5 | Alex Guerrieri | 46 | 3.2% | 47 / 2.6% | 1 | 2015-10-26 |
| 6 | Albert | 44 | 3.0% | 52 / 2.9% | 3 | 2016-10-19 |
| 7 | Robert Pająk | 41 | 2.8% | 42 / 2.3% | 4 | 2023-03-01 |
| 8 | Cameron Moore | 40 | 2.7% | 50 / 2.7% | 1 | 2016-09-27 |
| 9 | Andrew Austin | 37 | 2.5% | 48 / 2.6% | 1 | 2015-08-23 |
| 10 | Klaus Post | 28 | 1.9% | 35 / 1.9% | 1 | 2024-04-08 |
| 11 | majiayu000 | 24 | 1.6% | 27 / 1.5% | 1 | 2026-01-01 |
| 12 | Thimo | 15 | 1.0% | 15 / 0.8% | 1 | 2014-11-26 |

The other 32 authors hold 4.9% together; the largest of them is Martin Lindhe at 0.9%.

**Who comes next:** Linda_pp, at 7.6%, which is about one eighth of Fatih Arslan's share. Linda_pp's lines all come from a single 2017 commit, so they are not an active second maintainer. Matthew Hall (5.8%, one commit in March 2026) is the next most recent contributor.

**Bus factor** (the smallest number of authors whose surviving lines reach 50% of the lines, whole repo):
- **1** on non-blank lines, and also 1 when blank lines are counted. Fatih Arslan alone passes 50%.
- 6 authors are needed to reach 80% (5 if blank lines are counted).

The commit history gives the same picture: Fatih Arslan made 80 of 139 non-merge commits (57.6%), and their last commit was on 2026-03-20.

**What the numbers include and leave out:**
- **Left out:** 4 files with 237 lines in total:
  - the vendored code (2 files, 128 lines)
  - the generated `attribute_string.go` (103 lines)
  - `go.sum` (6 lines)
- **Bots:** dependabot[bot] is not credited (7 lines, 38 commits).
- **Ignored commit:** `4831a13` is skipped because the repo's `.git-blame-ignore-revs` lists it. It was a mechanical `gofmt` rewrite of `interface{}` to `any`, so those lines stay with whoever wrote them originally.
- **Other mechanical-looking commits:** the tool checked 97 commits that still hold lines. It flagged one more, `52aa0a6` by Leo Correa. I looked at it: it fixes a single typo (`fmt.Ffprintf` to `fmt.Fprintf`), so I kept it. It holds 1 line.
- **Copy detection** (`-C`) moved 85 lines in one file to a different author. Without it, Fatih Arslan has 56.6% instead of 59.5%, Raj Patel has 6.0% instead of 0.2%, and Alex Guerrieri has 1.6% instead of 3.2%. Without copy detection, Raj Patel would rank second at 6.0%, just ahead of Linda_pp.
- **Duplicate names:** two people show up under more than one name with the same address, and each is counted once. Greg Poirson also appears as "greg", and Albert also appears as "bogem".
- **Possible duplicate person:** Erik Hollensbe may appear under two addresses (<email redacted> and <email redacted>). The evidence is weak, so they are counted separately. Merged, they would have 9 lines (0.6%), which changes nothing in the ranking.

**Limits:** these numbers show whose code is still in the repo. They do not show code quality, review work, or who understands the code.

**Overall:** for a dependency, you are relying on one maintainer. Nearly all other contributors made a single drive-by commit, several of them years ago. Since Fatih Arslan's last commit was in March 2026, the practical risk depends on whether they keep maintaining it. A sensible precaution is to pin the version you use and be ready to fork it; the library is small (about 1,500 lines).

=== ANSWER B ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - Bash: cat .git-blame-ignore-revs; git ls-files | sed 's|/.*||' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head; git log --format='%h %an <%ae> %s' | head -30; git shortlog -sne HEAD | head
 - Bash: cat .mailmap 2>/dev/null; head -5 attribute_string.go; git show --stat dee565e | tail -5
b(){ for f in "$@"; do git blame -w -M -C -C --line-porcelain --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs -- "$f"; done | grep '^author-mail' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn; }
echo "== naive (all non-vendor, no flags)"; for f in $(git ls-files | grep -v '^vendor/'); do git blame --line-porcelain -- $f; done | grep '^author-mail' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
echo "== hand-written .go, moves+ignore-revs"; b $(git ls-files '*.go' | grep -v '^vendor/' | grep -v attribute_string.go)
echo "== all non-vendor non-go.sum, moves+ignore"; b $(git ls-files | grep -v '^vendor/' | grep -v go.sum)
 - Bash: git log --format='%an <%ae>' | grep -E 'rhysd|matt.hall' | sort -u; git ls-files vendor | head; for f in $(git ls-files | grep -v '^vendor/'); do git blame --line-porcelain -- $f; done | grep -c '^author-mail'; git log -1 --format=%ad 4c2cd34; git log --since=2024-01-01 --no-merges --format='%an' -- '*.go' ':!vendor' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
FINAL ANSWER:
**Short answer:** Fatih Arslan owns about **57%** of the hand-written Go code that is live today. Next is **Linda_pp (rhysd) at about 9%**, then **Matthew Hall at about 6%**. Everyone else is at 4% or less, across roughly 20 more contributors.

**How I counted:** I ran `git blame` on the current HEAD. It ignores whitespace, follows code that was moved or copied (`-M -C -C`), and skips the gofmt commit listed in `.git-blame-ignore-revs`. I left out `vendor/`, `go.sum` and the generated `attribute_string.go`.

| Rank | Author | Lines | Share |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Fatih Arslan | 893 / 1554 | 57.5% |
| 2 | Linda_pp (rhysd) | 136 | 8.8% |
| 3 | Matthew Hall | 98 | 6.3% |
| 4 | Greg Poirson | 64 | 4.1% |
| 5 | bogem | 52 | 3.3% |

**Why a quick count gives a different picture:** if you blame all non-vendor files without those options, Fatih drops to about 50% and **Raj Patel looks like #2 at about 8%**. That is misleading. Raj's lines come mostly from mechanical commits:
- `4831a13`: the `interface{}` → `any` gofmt rewrite.
- `dee565e`: moving `Attribute` into `attribute.go`.

Once you follow moved code and skip the gofmt commit, Raj has only 2 lines. Lena Hoffmann's 103 lines are all the generated `stringer` file, so they don't show real authorship either.

**What this means for your risk:**
- It is mostly one person's code. One maintainer wrote more than half of it, and no one else has more than about 9%.
- Recent activity shows the same pattern. Since 2024 there have been few non-merge Go commits, spread across about 10 people, each with 1–4 commits. Fatih merges all PRs. Most recent commits are dependabot dependency updates.
- This is a small, stable library (about 1.5k lines), so it doesn't need many maintainers. Your real exposure is that one person controls merging and releases, not that the code is complex.

